1
00:04:08,181 --> 00:04:14,181
Peter Fonda is riding again... To the woman he
lost... for the revenge he craves!

2
00:08:16,363 --> 00:08:22,363
The Hired Hand is a western film directed by Peter Fonda.

3
00:12:24,545 --> 00:12:30,545
Harry Collings returns home to his farm after drifting with his friend, Arch.

4
00:16:32,727 --> 00:16:38,727
His wife, who had given up on him, reluctantly allows him to
stay, and soon believes that all will be well again.

5
00:20:40,909 --> 00:20:46,909
But then Harry has to make a difficult decision
regarding his loyalties and priorities.

6
00:24:49,090 --> 00:24:55,090
Harry Collings, played by Peter Fonda.

7
00:28:57,272 --> 00:29:03,272
Arch Harris, played by Warren Oates.

8
00:33:05,454 --> 00:33:11,454
Hannah Collings, played by Verna Bloom.

9
00:37:13,636 --> 00:37:19,636
Dan Griffen, played by Robert Pratt.

10
00:41:21,818 --> 00:41:27,818
McVey, played by Severn Darden.

11
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:36,000
Mexican Woman, played by Rita Rogers.

12
00:49:38,181 --> 00:49:44,181
The Hired Hand is a 1971 American Western film directed by
Peter Fonda, with a screenplay by Alan Sharp.

13
00:53:46,363 --> 00:53:52,363
The film stars Fonda, Warren Oates, and Verna Bloom.

14
00:57:54,545 --> 00:58:00,545
The cinematography was by Vilmos Zsigmond.

15
01:02:02,727 --> 01:02:08,727
Bruce Langhorne provided the moody film score.

16
01:06:10,909 --> 01:06:16,909
The story is about a man returning to his abandoned wife after seven years of
drifting from job to job throughout the Southwestern United States.

17
01:10:19,090 --> 01:10:25,090
The embittered woman will only let him stay if he
agrees to move in as a hired hand.

18
01:14:27,272 --> 01:14:33,272
Upon release, the film received a mixed critical
response and was a financial failure.

19
01:18:35,454 --> 01:18:41,454
In 1973, the film was shown on NBC-TV in an expanded
version, but soon drifted into obscurity.

20
01:22:43,636 --> 01:22:49,636
In 2001, a fully restored version was shown at various film festivals, gaining strong
critical praise, and it was released by the Sundance Channel on DVD.

21
01:26:51,818 --> 01:26:57,818
It is now considered a classic Western of the period.
